@startuml Interface_Endpoint_PrivateLink_SQS
title Interface VPC Endpoint (PrivateLink): EC2 -> SQS using the PUBLIC URL\n(VPC 10.0.0.0/16, us-east-1, Private DNS = ON)

autonumber
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign left
skinparam responseMessageBelowArrow true

box "Your VPC 10.0.0.0/16" #EEF4FF
  participant "App on EC2\n10.0.1.10\n(private subnet, us-east-1a)\nSG: sg-app" as App
  participant "Route 53 Resolver\n10.0.0.2 (VPC base +2)" as DNS
  participant "AWS-managed\nPrivate Hosted Zone\nsqs.us-east-1.amazonaws.com" as PHZ
  participant "VPC Router\n(route table)\n10.0.0.0/16 -> local\n0.0.0.0/0 -> nat-gw" as RT
  participant "Endpoint ENI\nvpce-0abc\n10.0.1.25 (1a)\n[10.0.2.40 (1b)]\nSG: sg-vpce" as ENI
end box

participant "PrivateLink\n(Hyperplane, built-in)\n+ Endpoint Policy" as PL

box "AWS Service" #FFF4E5
  participant "Amazon SQS\nus-east-1" as SQS
end box

participant "NAT GW / IGW\n(NOT used)" as NAT #LightGray

== One-time setup (admin) ==
note over App, PL
  Create interface endpoint: service com.amazonaws.us-east-1.sqs
  - Subnets: one per AZ -> ENIs 10.0.1.25 (1a), 10.0.2.40 (1b)
  - Security group sg-vpce: inbound TCP 443 from sg-app
  - Private DNS: ON (needs VPC enableDnsSupport + enableDnsHostnames)
  - Endpoint policy: allow sqs:SendMessage on orders-queue
  AWS then attaches a hidden private hosted zone to the VPC
  that overrides sqs.us-east-1.amazonaws.com.
  The ENIs are "requester-managed": you cannot route to or modify them.
end note

== Step 1-3: DNS turns the PUBLIC name into a PRIVATE IP ==
App -> App : SDK builds request to\nhttps://sqs.us-east-1.amazonaws.com\n(public URL, no code change)
App -> DNS : DNS query: sqs.us-east-1.amazonaws.com ?
DNS -> PHZ : Is there a private zone for this name\nattached to this VPC?
PHZ --> DNS : Yes: A 10.0.1.25, 10.0.2.40
DNS --> App : 10.0.1.25, 10.0.2.40
note right of DNS
  Same name asked from the internet
  returns public IPs (52.46.x.x).
  Inside this VPC it returns ENI IPs.
  THIS is the key step.
end note

== Step 4-6: Local routing + security groups ==
App -> RT : TCP SYN to 10.0.1.25:443\n(sg-app outbound 443 allowed)
RT -> RT : 10.0.1.25 is in 10.0.0.0/16\n-> match "local" route
RT ->x NAT : 0.0.0.0/0 route NOT used\n(no NAT data charges, no internet)
RT -> ENI : Deliver to 10.0.1.25
ENI -> ENI : sg-vpce inbound: 443 from sg-app? -> allowed

== Step 7: ENI -> PrivateLink (built-in, no configuration) ==
ENI -> PL : Packet picked up by Hyperplane\nfrom the VPC network fabric
PL -> PL : NAT addresses (why overlapping CIDRs are fine)\nEvaluate endpoint policy: sqs:SendMessage allowed?
PL -> SQS : Forward over AWS private backbone

== Step 8-9: TLS + IAM authorization at the service ==
App <-> SQS : TLS handshake: SQS certificate is for\nsqs.us-east-1.amazonaws.com\n-> matches the hostname the app used -> valid
App -> SQS : SendMessage (SigV4 signed with instance role creds)
SQS -> SQS : IAM: instance role allows sqs:SendMessage?\nQueue policy condition aws:SourceVpce = vpce-0abc?
note right of SQS
  Optional lockdown: queue policy
  Deny unless aws:SourceVpce = vpce-0abc
  -> queue reachable ONLY via this endpoint.
end note

== Step 10: Response returns the same path ==
SQS --> PL : 200 OK (MessageId)
PL --> ENI : (connection tracked by Hyperplane)
ENI --> App : 200 OK via local route

note over App, NAT
  Summary:
  - URL stays PUBLIC; DNS answer becomes PRIVATE (private hosted zone)
  - Route table: "local" route reaches the ENI; NAT/IGW never touched
  - ENI -> PrivateLink is built in (Hyperplane); you only control DNS, SGs, endpoint policy
  - If Private DNS is OFF: public name -> public IP -> traffic goes via NAT again;
    you must use vpce-0abc.sqs.us-east-1.vpce.amazonaws.com instead
end note

@enduml
